= Semiotics — Simple =

'''Semiotics''' is the study of signs and how they come to represent, indicate, or communicate something. Words are signs, but signs can also be images, gestures, sounds, symptoms, diagrams, symbols, or other things that people or systems interpret.

A stop sign is more than a red metal shape. Drivers learn a convention that connects that shape with an instruction. Smoke can also function as a sign of fire even when nobody intentionally produced it as a message. Semiotics studies these different ways that signs work.

== Two major traditions ==

Modern semiotics was strongly influenced by two thinkers who developed different theories.

'''Charles Sanders Peirce''' treated signification as a three-part relation involving a sign, an object, and an interpretant—the effect or further sign through which the first sign is understood.<ref name="peirce">[https://plato.stanford.edu/archives/sum2010/entries/peirce-semiotics/ Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy, “Peirce's Theory of Signs.”]</ref>

Peirce also distinguished:

* '''icons''', which signify partly through resemblance;
* '''indices''', which signify through an actual connection;
* '''symbols''', which depend strongly on rules, habits, or conventions.

A photograph can have both iconic and indexical features, so these categories are not always exclusive.

'''Ferdinand de Saussure''' approached signs through linguistics. His tradition emphasizes the relation between a signifier and a signified and the way signs gain value through contrasts within a [[Language]] system.

The two traditions overlap, but their concepts are not interchangeable.

== Syntax, semantics and pragmatics ==

Charles Morris later described three dimensions of sign systems:

* '''syntactics''' — relations among signs;
* [[Semantics]] — relations between signs and what they designate;
* [[Pragmatics]] — relations between signs and their users or interpreters.<ref name="morris">Morris, C. W. (1938), ''Foundations of the Theory of Signs''.</ref>

In actual communication these dimensions interact. A sentence can be grammatically structured, refer to something, and still have different effects depending on who says it and in what situation.

== Signs are not the same as signals ==

A physical [[Signal]] can carry a sign, but successful transmission does not guarantee shared meaning.

For example, a computer can transmit the same sequence of bytes perfectly while two people interpret the resulting message differently. [[Information]] in the mathematical sense therefore does not automatically explain semantic meaning.

Similarly, a [[Code]] can specify exact correspondences between symbols, but human languages and cultural signs often involve ambiguity, history, metaphor, and context that cannot be reduced to a simple codebook.

== How semiotics is studied ==

Researchers may analyze texts, images, media, gestures, interfaces, rituals, or cultural practices. They can compare sign systems, study historical changes, observe how communities interpret signs, or experimentally test how participants respond to particular representations.

Evidence matters. An analyst's possible interpretation is not automatically the meaning shared by everyone in a culture.

== Culture, biology and computation ==

Semiotics has been extended into many areas.

Cultural semiotics studies larger systems of meaning. Juri Lotman's [[Semiosphere]] describes environments in which many sign processes and cultural texts interact.

Biosemiotics examines sign-like processes in living systems. This can be useful for studying signaling and recognition, but a biological response does not by itself prove conscious interpretation.

Semiotic ideas are also applied to computer interfaces and artificial intelligence. Software can manipulate representations and produce meaningful outputs for users, but this fact alone does not demonstrate that the machine understands signs in the same way a person does.

